<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirabeau B. Lamar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mirabeau Buonaparte Lamar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirabeau B. Lamar was a Georgia-born politician and soldier who was the second President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, from 1838 to 1841. He fought at San Jacinto in 1836, moved the capital to the new city of Austin and promoted public education. His wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>, heavy borrowing and the failed Santa Fe Expedition left the republic deeply in debt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar.jpg" alt="Mirabeau B. Lamar" width="298" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author; derivative work by Oldag07 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirabeaulamar_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lamar was born on 16 August 1798 in Louisville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and grew up on Fairfield, his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantation near Milledgeville. His uncle Zachariah named him after the comte de Mirabeau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>. He was accepted at Princeton but did not attend, failed as a merchant and a newspaper owner, and in 1823 became private secretary to Governor George M. Troup of Georgia. He then founded the Columbus Enquirer, served one term in the Georgia Senate, lost his wife Tabitha to tuberculosis in 1830, and was admitted to the bar in 1833.</p><p>Grieving his brother Lucius, Lamar reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in 1835. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a>'s army in 1836 and, after rescuing two surrounded men on the eve of San Jacinto, was promoted from private to colonel and given the cavalry for the battle. He became Secretary of War in the interim government and was elected Vice President of Texas in 1836.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Inaugurated on 1 December 1838, Lamar told Congress that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> should be driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> even if they had to be destroyed, and he held that their total extinction was needed to open the land to whites. In 1839 Texian troops expelled the Cherokee at the Battle of the Neches, where about 500 Texans attacked some 800 people of several tribes, between 400 and 500 of them women, children and elders; Chief Bowles was killed. The Comanche resisted despite heavy losses.</p><p>He opposed annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. A commission chose the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> as the capital, renamed Austin, and he founded the Texas State Library. Peace talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and recognition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> failed. To fill the treasury he issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, the Redbacks, which proved nearly worthless, and in 1841 he sent an expedition to Santa Fe without Congress's approval; its members were captured and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and many died. The public debt rose from $1.4 million to almost $7 million. He also set aside land for schools and two universities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lamar fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> in the Mexican–American War, sat in the Texas Legislature for Eagle Pass, and in 1857 was appointed minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. He published his poems as Verse Memorials the same year. He died of a heart attack at his Richmond plantation on 19 December 1859. Lamar County and Lamar University in Beaumont are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirabeau B. Lamar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sam Houston negotiated peace with them in the late 1830s, but <b>Mirabeau B. Lamar</b> reversed that policy in 1838 and ordered an invasion of Comancheria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Louisville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Richmond, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33,-82.41&amp;year=1837" aria-label="Louisville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2440 965 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mirabeau B. Lamar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2890 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2890 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33,-82.41&amp;year=1837">What was here in 1837? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
